---
/**
 * Trojan libration — explainer for "Searching for Exotrojans in Pulsar Systems".
 *
 * The paper found no Trojans. This figure shows what one would have looked like,
 * and says so in a standing line of text that cannot be collapsed or dismissed.
 *
 * Static SVG below is the whole figure for print, Reader mode, screen readers and
 * no-JS. The canvas simulation is additional: it is hidden until the script runs,
 * so nothing shows a dead control.
 *
 * The simulation is the circular restricted three-body problem in the rotating
 * frame, integrated with RK4 at a fixed step decoupled from the frame rate. Mass
 * ratio mu = 0.01 / 1.41, well under the 0.0385 limit, so libration about L4 is
 * genuinely stable. Preset behaviour was checked by integrating 60 orbits: the
 * Jacobi constant holds to machine precision for the trapped cases.
 */

// Geometry of the static diagram, in SVG units. Model units: the pulsar sits at
// the origin of the diagram's left body, the companion one separation away.
const CY = 160;
const PX = 169.2; // pulsar
const COX = 279.2; // companion
const L4X = 224.2;
const L4Y = 64.7;
const L5Y = 255.3;

// One full libration cycle starting 20 degrees off L4, integrated at h = 0.005.
// TODO: the "about four orbits" figure quoted in .geocap and in the written
// description is the small-amplitude limit. Re-integrating this mu at 20 degrees gives
// 4.2 orbits (5 degrees gives 4.45; the analytic limit is 4.57). "About four" would be
// closer for the loop actually drawn here.
const TADPOLE =
  'M188.3 51.7 L188.3 51.5 L188.6 51.1 L189.6 50.4 L192.0 49.7 L195.7 49.4 L200.5 49.7 ' +
  'L205.9 50.9 L211.1 52.8 L215.9 55.1 L220.1 57.4 L224.0 59.6 L227.8 61.6 L232.0 63.8 ' +
  'L236.6 66.5 L241.3 69.8 L245.6 73.8 L248.9 78.0 L250.9 81.9 L251.2 84.7 L249.9 86.1 ' +
  'L247.5 85.8 L244.3 84.2 L240.9 81.7 L237.6 78.9 L234.4 76.2 L231.2 73.9 L227.5 71.8 ' +
  'L223.2 69.8 L218.1 67.5 L212.5 65.0 L206.9 62.3 L202.1 59.8 L198.4 57.6 L195.8 56.0 ' +
  'L193.9 54.9 L192.3 54.3 L190.5 54.0 L188.6 53.6 L186.7 52.9 Z';

const presets = [
  {
    id: 'l4',
    label: 'At the stable point',
    deg: 0,
    words:
      'The asteroid starts exactly on the point. It stays at sixty degrees and does not move in this frame. The pulsar traces the same small shift once per orbit, over and over.',
  },
  {
    id: 'd5',
    label: '5° off',
    deg: 5,
    words:
      'The asteroid starts five degrees off the point. It swings between about fifty-five and sixty-six degrees and takes roughly four orbits to complete one swing. The pulsar trace gains a slow rise and fall on top of its once-per-orbit motion.',
  },
  {
    id: 'd20',
    label: '20° off',
    deg: 20,
    words:
      'The asteroid starts twenty degrees off the point. It swings between about forty-two and eighty-two degrees, still taking about four orbits per swing, and the loop it traces is the tadpole shape. The slow rise and fall in the pulsar trace is much larger.',
  },
  {
    id: 'far',
    label: 'Far away',
    deg: 120,
    words:
      'The asteroid starts a hundred and twenty degrees off the point, which is too far to be held. It drifts all the way around the orbit, and its distance from the pulsar changes a lot. The pulsar trace wanders and never repeats.',
  },
];
const START = 'd20';
const startWords = presets.find((p) => p.id === START)!.words;
---

<figure class="ex" data-explainer="trojan-libration">
  <figcaption>
    <b class="ex-title">How a Trojan moves, and what it would do to the pulsar</b>
    <p>
      Two bodies in orbit make two places, sixty degrees ahead of and behind the
      smaller one, where a third object can stay put. Thousands of asteroids sit
      in those places in the Sun–Jupiter system. An object parked there swings
      slowly around the point, and its pull shifts the pulsar by a small amount.
      We didn't find a Trojan around any of the pulsars we searched. The figure below
      shows how one moves and the shift it gives the pulsar.
    </p>
  </figcaption>

  <svg class="geo" viewBox="0 0 340 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="tl-t tl-d">
    <title id="tl-t">Where a Trojan sits, and the loop it traces</title>
    <desc id="tl-d">
      The pulsar on the left, the companion on the right, one separation apart.
      Sixty degrees around from the companion, as seen from the pulsar, is the
      point L4, which makes an equal-sided triangle with the two of them. An
      asteroid near L4 traces the closed loop drawn around it. L5 is the matching
      point on the other side.
    </desc>

    <circle class="orbit" cx={PX} cy={CY} r={COX - PX}></circle>
    <polygon class="tri" points={`${PX},${CY} ${COX},${CY} ${L4X},${L4Y}`}></polygon>
    <path class="arc" d={`M${PX + 44} ${CY} A44 44 0 0 0 ${PX + 22} ${CY - 38.1}`}></path>
    <text class="tiny" x={PX + 48} y={CY - 18}>60°</text>

    <path class="tad" d={TADPOLE}></path>
    <g class="mark">
      <line x1={L4X - 6} y1={L4Y} x2={L4X + 6} y2={L4Y}></line>
      <line x1={L4X} y1={L4Y - 6} x2={L4X} y2={L4Y + 6}></line>
      <line x1={L4X - 6} y1={L5Y} x2={L4X + 6} y2={L5Y}></line>
      <line x1={L4X} y1={L5Y - 6} x2={L4X} y2={L5Y + 6}></line>
    </g>
    <text class="plbl" x={L4X} y={L4Y - 22} text-anchor="middle">L4</text>
    <text class="plbl" x={L4X} y={L5Y + 26} text-anchor="middle">L5</text>

    <circle class="comp" cx={COX} cy={CY} r="9"></circle>
    <circle class="puls" cx={PX} cy={CY} r="6"></circle>
    <circle class="ast" cx="188.3" cy="51.7" r="4.5"></circle>
    <text class="tiny" x={PX} y={CY + 24} text-anchor="middle">pulsar</text>
    <text class="tiny" x={COX} y={CY + 26} text-anchor="middle">companion</text>

  </svg>

  <ul class="key">
    <li><span class="sw puls"></span>the pulsar, about 1.4 solar masses</li>
    <li><span class="sw comp"></span>the companion, about 0.01 solar masses</li>
    <li><span class="sw ast"></span>the asteroid</li>
    <li><span class="sw line"></span>the loop it traces</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="geocap">
    This is drawn in a frame that turns once per orbit, so the companion stays put and
    only the asteroid moves. The loop is the path of a Trojan started twenty
    degrees off L4 and followed for one complete swing, which takes about four orbits
    of the binary.
  </p>

  <noscript>
    <p class="note">
      The animated version of this figure needs JavaScript. The diagram above and the
      written description below cover the same idea.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class="sim" id="tl-sim" hidden>
    <div class="group" role="group" aria-label="Where the asteroid starts">
      <span class="glabel">Where the asteroid starts</span>
      <div class="btns">
        {presets.map((p) => (
          <button type="button" class="preset" data-preset={p.id} aria-pressed={p.id === START}>
            {p.label}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="rmnote" id="tl-rm" hidden>
      Your system is set to reduce motion, so nothing here moves by itself. Step
      forward advances the simulation a quarter of an orbit each time you press
      it. Play still works if you would rather watch it run.
    </p>

    <div class="btns run">
      <button type="button" id="tl-play" aria-pressed="false">Play</button>
      <button type="button" id="tl-step">Step forward</button>
      <button type="button" id="tl-reset">Back to the start</button>
    </div>

    <div class="canvases">
      <canvas id="tl-orbit" class="orbit-c" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      <p class="striplbl">What the telescope measures: the pulsar's own small shift, along the line of sight</p>
      <canvas id="tl-strip" class="strip-c" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      <p class="tinyp">
        Faint vertical lines mark one orbit of the binary. The Trojan causes
        both the part that repeats every orbit and the slow swell on top of it. The
        repeating part blends into the orbit itself, and the slow swell is what a search
        can pick out.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="status" id="tl-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">{startWords}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="honesty">
    <p>
      The asteroid's motion is a three-body calculation using the mass ratio from the
      paper, a companion of about 0.01 solar masses against a 1.4 solar-mass pulsar.
      The sizes of the bodies and the height of the trace at the bottom are just for
      the picture.
    </p>
  </div>

  <details class="describe">
    <summary>Describe this figure in words</summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The diagram uses the rotating frame described above. The pulsar
        is the small filled circle on the left. The companion, about a hundredth
        of a solar mass, is the larger circle on the right. The dashed circle is
        the companion's orbit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sixty degrees around from the companion, measured at the pulsar, is the
        point marked L4. The dashed triangle joining the pulsar, the companion, and
        L4 has three equal sides.
        L5 is the mirror image of it below the line. Both are places where the
        pull of the two bodies and the turning of the frame cancel out.
      </p>
      <p>
        An object placed exactly at L4 stays there. Place it a little off and it
        swings around the point and comes back.
        The closed loop drawn around L4 is one of those swings, for an object
        started twenty degrees away. The shape is why these are called tadpole
        orbits. Going around the loop once takes about four orbits of
        the binary, so the motion is slow compared with the orbit it sits in.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start it far enough from the point and it is not held at all. It drifts
        around the whole orbit, its distance from the pulsar changing as it goes,
        and never settles into a loop around sixty degrees.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strip below the animation shows the pulsar's own motion.
        The asteroid pulls on the pulsar, so the pulsar is not quite where
        it would be without it, and that shows up as a change in when its pulses
        arrive. The trace repeats once per orbit, and a
        signal at exactly the orbital period is difficult to separate from the
        orbit itself, since fitting the binary absorbs it. The slow swell riding on top, at the libration period, is the part that would
        give a Trojan away, and it is what we looked for.
      </p>
      </div>
  </details>

  <script type="application/json" data-tl-presets is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify({ presets, start: START })} />
</figure>

<style>
